  "generated_text": "To find the modulo \\(6\\) remainder of the sum \\(1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + 99 + 100\\), we start by using the formula for the sum of the first \\(n\\) positive integers:\n\n\\[\nS = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(n = 100\\). Plugging in the value of \\(n\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nS = \\frac{100 \\cdot 101}{2} = 50 \\cdot 101\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the remainder when \\(50 \\cdot 101\\) is divided by \\(6\\). We will break this down into simpler steps.\n\nFirst, let's find the remainders of \\(50\\) and \\(101\\) when divided by \\(6\\):\n\n\\[\n50 \\div 6 = 8 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 50 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n101 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 5 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 101 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the product \\(50 \\cdot 101\\) modulo \\(6\\):\n\n\\[\n50 \\cdot 101 \\equiv 2 \\cdot 5 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\nCalculate the product:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 5 = 10\n\\]\n\nFinally, find the remainder when \\(10\\) is divided by \\(6\\):\n\n\\[\n10 \\div 6 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 10 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\nThus, the modulo \\(6\\) remainder of the sum \\(1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + 99 + 100\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
